Done. Here's what I did and verified:

**Change** (`swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:18-58`):
- Added `public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable { case malformedFormat }`
- Added `public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note`
- Added a private `noteFieldSeparator = "\n---\n"` constant shared with the format

**Behavior:**
- Splits on the literal `\n---\n` with `omittingEmptySubsequences: false`, so it counts *exactly* 4 parts (empty fields still count as their part).
- If `parts.count != 4`, throws `NoteParseError.malformedFormat` — no guessing, no defaulting, no crash.
- Otherwise maps `parts[0]`→id, `parts[1]`→title, `parts[2]`→pinned (`"1"`→true, anything else→false), `parts[3]`→body, and builds tags via the existing `extractTags(from:)`.

**Verified:**
- `swiftc -typecheck` passes (fixed the initializer arg order to match `Note`'s `id:title:body:tags:pinned` signature).
- Standalone smoke test confirms: valid note parses correctly (id/title/pinned/tags/body), and 3/empty/5-part inputs all throw `malformedFormat` as expected.

**Not verifiable here:** `swift test` / `swift build` fail with `sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted` — an environment restriction on this machine, unrelated to the code (the manifest compiles fine; only the sandbox can't be invoked). The direct `swiftc -typecheck` plus the runtime smoke test cover the logic.

One judgment call worth flagging: I made the separator a private file-level `let` rather than hardcoding it in two places, so the parse format stays a single source of truth.


